WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.100
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm revealing the very best

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.459
Karate Kid easter eggs, epic call-backs, and
hidden details you might have missed in the

00:00:09.459 --> 00:00:11.449
third season of Cobra Kai!

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:14.020
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.570
Before they came up with Eagle Fang as the
name for Johnny's new dojo,

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.699
"Welcome to Eagle Fang Karate."

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:23.499
"Uh, eagles don't have fang.."
the show's writers tossed around various suggestions

00:00:23.499 --> 00:00:28.249
for different animals that his character would
think of as representing strength and masculinity,

00:00:28.249 --> 00:00:29.320
including lions.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.220
The final idea came from Johnny's "80s mentality
and the eagle that he has on the wall of his

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.220
apartment."

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.290
And I imagine something that also influenced
the name was Johnny's love of a particular

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.430
80s military action movie that we see him
watch repeatedly:

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.550
"What are your likes?"

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.570
"I like muscle cars and Iron Eagle.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:46.570
And Iron Eagle II."

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.590
As for the "Fang" part of the dojo title,
the showrunners say that's "just consistent

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.850
with [Johnny's] ignorance" and "he thinks
it sounds badass."

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.400
"You gotta swoop down like an eagle, grab
'em with your claws, and sink your fangs into

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.400
'em."

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.520
When the Country Club's kitchen staff start
dishing out spaghetti and red sauce,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.600
"Don't skimp on that sauce."

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.830
it sets up a call-back to the original Karate
Kid movie where Daniel gets covered in that

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.960
same food after bumping into a waiter when
he sneaks into the Country Club kitchen and

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.680
sees Johnny kissing Ali on the dancefloor.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:18.469
This time though, the boot's on the other
foot, as it's Johnny who arrives at the Club

00:01:18.469 --> 00:01:21.270
for a date with Ali to find her and Daniel
together.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:26.210
But, luckily for him, unlike young Daniel,
adult Johnny narrowly avoids a run-in with

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.439
the red sauce:
"Hey, watch it!

00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:29.439
I'm wearing white here.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:31.090
You think I want that all over me?"

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.149
And when Johnny makes fun of Daniel's suit,
his rival's comeback

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:38.549
"You should talk there, Scarface."
is a reference to Al Pacino's famous movie

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:45.170
role as penniless Cuban Tony Montana who becomes
a drug lord nicknamed Scarface in 1980s Miami.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.340
The way Ali greets Daniel when she sees him,
for the first time in decades, at the Country

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.659
Club
"Daniel with an L!"

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:54.649
is a nod to this scene in the original movie:
"Hey, you got a name?"

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:57.240
"Ali, with an I.
Hey, what's your name?"

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.579
"Daniel, with an L."
And Tory does a similar thing in season 2:

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:04.060
"Name's Miguel."

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.310
"Tory.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.310
With a Y."

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.679
When the LaRussos invite Cobra Kai's landlord
over for dinner to ask him to stop renting

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:15.569
the dojo to Kreese, the camera starts the
scene by focusing in on a dish of macaroni

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:16.569
and cheese.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:17.920
"Macaroni and cheese?

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.510
You know what I like!

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:24.060
I grew up on macaroni and cheese."

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:28.800
The skeleton hoodie Tory wears during Cobra
Kai's brutal payback attack on Miyagi-Do is

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.879
a hat-tip to the skeleton costumes Johnny
and his crew wore to the Halloween dance

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:37.180
and while they were beating up Daniel afterwards
and then getting their butts kicked by Mr

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.870
Miyagi in the original movie.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:43.030
And that skeleton costume actually popped
up in Season One when Johnny gave it to Miguel

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.650
to wear for his Halloween dance.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:49.330
And, like Johnny all those years earlier,
Miguel also ended up in a fight that night.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.131
In both the TV show and the original movie,
the beef between Cobra Kai and Miyagi-do also

00:02:54.131 --> 00:02:55.939
plays out on the soccer pitch.

00:02:55.939 --> 00:03:00.760
Dimitri deliberately trips Hawk up, just like
Bobby did to Daniel in the first movie,

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.300
and curiously, the music you hear during the
match in the series is "Rock 'n' Roll Over

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.640
You", which featured in the second film's
ice-breaking scene.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.540
One of the fun things about Cobra Kai is getting
to see parts of the Karate Kid movies from

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.820
a different perspective
and it seems like there's a shout-out at the

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.790
school board meeting to the popular satirical
YouTube video by J Matthew Turner about Danny

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:28.319
being the real villain of The Karate Kid:
"When I went to school here, I was bullied,

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:29.329
and karate saved me."

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:31.129
"I heard you were the real bully!"

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:35.140
That's also a nod to the first season where
Johnny claimed to Miguel that he was victimised

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.140
by Daniel.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:37.780
"Out of nowhere, the guy sucker punches me."

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:38.780
"Yeah!

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:39.780
Yeah!"

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:40.780
"What an asshole."

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:41.780
"I know, man."

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.270
Though this season, Daniel gives Miguel his
version of events.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.470
"All that I knew was that he broke her radio.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.470
She was upset.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:48.530
I was being chivalrous."

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.569
And speaking of different sides to the story,
it turns out that Daniel's reason for his

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:55.300
and Ali's break-up,
"She tells me that she's just fallen in love

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.430
with some football player from UCLA",
was rather different to Ali's version:

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:04.840
"So I got into UCLA, and I ran into this guy
that I knew who went there.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.680
Daniel sees me talking to my friend..

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.400
He jumps to conclusions."

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.590
"I thought you were in love with the guy."

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:14.270
And they didn't see eye-to-eye on Daniel's
'47 Ford either, with Daniel complaining to

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.500
Mr Miyagi that:
"I let Ali borrow the car, and she redesigns

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.500
my fender.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:20.880
And I don't know what she did to the engine,
but that ain't running right, either."

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.310
Whereas, according to Ali:
"I told you that the brakes on Mr. Miyagi's

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.460
car were gonna go.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.419
I hope you didn't tell him that it was my
fault it crashed?"

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:30.139
"No, of course not."

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:34.830
And when Ali calls Daniel and Johnny out over
their relentless bickering, she neatly sums

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:40.050
up the premise of the entire Cobra Kai show:
"You say one thing, you say the opposite.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:42.390
You both think there's only one side to the
story.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.940
There's your side, and your side, and then
there is the truth."

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:50.389
Talking of different viewpoints, the show
continues to include unused footage from the

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:55.270
original movies that's never been seen before,
as they did in the very first scene in season

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:56.270
one.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.750
This season, in the flashbacks to Chozen's
final fight with Daniel, when Miyagi impresses

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:05.419
on him how serious the situation is, Daniel
replying 'I know' has been added to the scene.

00:05:05.419 --> 00:05:06.960
"This not tournament.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.500
This for real."

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:09.520
"I know."

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.220
When Daniel goes to Cobra Kai looking for
Johnny at the start of the season but finds

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:16.770
Kreese there instead,
as he enters the space he sees the phrase

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.190
"Fear Does Not Exist In This Dojo" painted
on the wall where previously Johnny's Cobra

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:22.380
Kai logo had been.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.870
Those were Kreese's very first words in the
original Karate Kid movie that Danny heard

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:28.920
when he visited his dojo for the first time.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:32.880
"Fear does not exist in this dojo, does it?"

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:33.949
"No, sensei!"

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:38.210
Adding to Danny's sense of déjà vu is a
standee of the older Kreese, similar to the

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.699
one he saw of the younger villain in the film.

00:05:40.699 --> 00:05:45.360
In the present day, Kreese delights in rubbing
in the fact that Miguel was put in a coma

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.090
by a kid taught by Daniel.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:51.789
And he uses a Mr Miyagi-ism to make the point:
"But I don't really blame Robby.

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:55.449
'Cause you know what they say...
there's no such thing as a bad student."

00:05:55.449 --> 00:05:57.740
"No such thing bad student.

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:58.960
Only bad teacher."

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.430
Season 3 fills us in on Kreese's backstory.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:06.030
And the first flashback to his youth is deliberately
set up to make us think at first that this

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.930
guy here is actually the young Kreese.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:08.930
"They're the opponent.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.020
You don't show them mercy."

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.879
And it's only when the busboy's employer shouts
at him, that we realise who he really is:

00:06:15.879 --> 00:06:16.879
"Kreese!

00:06:16.879 --> 00:06:18.160
The tables are not gonna bus themselves.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.160
Get back to work!"

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:23.250
And a neat little inside joke that adds to
this scene's fake-out is that the guy we initially

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:28.340
think is Kreese is, in fact, played by Jesse
Kove, the son of actor Martin Kove, who plays

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.710
the adult Kreese.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.770
The showrunners intentionally framed Kreese
as a "typical underdog" in this flashback

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.330
as they wanted us to see him in a different
light because, as they told EW,

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:40.560
"one of the [show's] central themes [...] is
redemption and looking back at these black-and-white

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.150
characters and trying to find the gray."

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:47.350
Another neat detail about that scene is that
it kicks off with the same kind of car that

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:51.110
Mr Miyagi gifted Daniel in the first film,
a '47 Ford.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.849
"Piece of junk!

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:54.190
Scrap this and buy some muscle."

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.620
Although the license plate is different to
Daniel's, I wouldn't be surprised if they're

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.000
supposed to be the same car, perhaps saved
from the scrapyard.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.789
After all, that's how Eric got his ride in
the franchise's fourth film:

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:06.020
"Hey, do you like my car?

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.220
I saved it from the junkyard."

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:12.000
The old article about Kreese from the 80s
that Robbie finds online includes a quote

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:15.050
from him that "Second and third place don't
count.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:19.810
Which echoes his mantra from the first film,
"Defeat does not exist in this dojo",

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.759
and recalls his treatment of Johnny in the
second one:

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:23.759
"Second place?

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:24.759
Second place is no place!

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:25.759
You're off the team!"

00:07:25.759 --> 00:07:28.800
And, as we see in the finale when he suggests
settling everything in the tournament, Kreese

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.960
is still goading Johnny about that second
place back in '84 all these years later, because,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.120
notice how he looks at Johnny when he says
"if you lose":

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:38.290
"If you lose..."

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.460
"We won't lose."

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:43.930
And the second article that Robbie pulls up
about how Miyagi's dojo "defanged" Kreese's

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.090
Cobras has the same title as the newspaper
clipping that Kreese picked up then tossed

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:49.650
aside in the third movie.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:54.449
Speaking of Johnny's Facebook photos, those
shirtless shots that Miguel tactfully turns

00:07:54.449 --> 00:07:58.979
down for his sensei's new profile,
are actually real photos taken of William

00:07:58.979 --> 00:08:02.790
Zabka for teen magazines during his days as
a teen idol.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.490
The showrunners had been "ready to recreate"
a few shots, but as Zabka had "some photos

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.759
on hand that worked" they went with those.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:14.729
When Chozen first shows up, his angry demeanour
suggests he's still holding a grudge against

00:08:14.729 --> 00:08:19.569
Daniel for humiliating him in the second film,
and you can tell by Daniel's clenched fist

00:08:19.569 --> 00:08:25.949
that he hasn't forgotten Mr Miyagi's words:
"Daniel-san, in Okinawa, honor have no time

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:26.949
limit."

00:08:26.949 --> 00:08:31.300
And the way Chozen interrupts Daniel and Kumiko
just after their hands lightly brush against

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:35.500
each other at the bar mirrors how, in the
movie, he disturbed them just as they started

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.339
to hold hands on their date.

00:08:37.339 --> 00:08:42.620
With actor Yuji Okumoto back as Chozen, the
show tips its hat to several of his and Daniel's

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:47.130
tussles from the sequel, including humorously
reversing their roles in their big fight scene

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.089
compared to the film.

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:50.089
"Honk."

00:08:50.089 --> 00:08:53.450
And later, when Chozen gives Daniel one of
Mr Miyagi's scrolls,

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.190
"You keep for your collection."

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:59.810
that's a riff on the scene in the movie where
Kumiko pelts Chozen with a tomato after he

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.400
attacks Daniel:
"You keep for your collection.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:04.100
I know you like it."

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:08.170
When Chozen shows Daniel around his dojo,
you can see several pictures there that are

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.330
the same as ones Daniel hung up in his dojo
last season.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:16.550
In fact, this photo here is actually a real-life
martial artist from Okinawa who founded the

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.340
Gōjū-ryū school of karate.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:21.930
And it's especially fitting that he appears
in the show because Robert Mark Kamen who

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:25.850
created the original Karate Kid characters
and wrote the first three movies trained in

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:30.180
that particular method and his instructor
learned it from the founder himself whose

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.420
photo we see here.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:37.090
During their catch-up in Okinawa, Kumiko reveals
why London's her favourite place she's ever

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.500
visited:
"I got to see the Cranberries.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:43.899
They played "Zombie," "Linger," and "Dream."

00:09:43.899 --> 00:09:47.100
That's a nod to the franchise's fourth movie,
The Next Karate Kid,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:51.450
where Hilary Swank's character Julie practises
her karate moves to the sounds of The Cranberries'

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.760
song Dreams and the monks at the Buddhist
monastery where she's staying join in dancing!

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:59.639
"Never trust spiritual leader who cannot dance."

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:02.810
And when Kumiko tells Daniel that she never
married but stayed

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.971
"a free agent",
that's a reference to how Daniel told her

00:10:05.971 --> 00:10:07.880
he was single in the movie:
"Are you arranged?"

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:08.930
"What, are you kidding?

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:09.990
I'm a free agent."

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.040
"What is free agent?"

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.890
"Well, that just means that I'm available."

00:10:12.890 --> 00:10:17.360
Curiously, after she left the dance company,
her life somewhat parallels her aunt Yukie's

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.240
who also taught O-Bon dance in Okinawa and
chose not to wed after her young love Mr Miyagi

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:23.850
left:
"Why you never marry?"

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.670
"You never come back."

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:28.810
"I'm supposed to believe a guy never got a
ring on that finger?"

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.620
"None of them fought to the death for me."

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.750
Which is, of course, a reference to how Chozen
took her hostage to force Daniel to fight

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:36.750
him.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:41.139
Traci Toguchi who played the little girl Yuna
in the second film also returns.

00:10:41.139 --> 00:10:45.870
And just as Daniel saved her during the typhoon
all those decades ago, thanks to her position

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:50.089
as the Senior Vice President of Sales for
Doyona she's able to return the favour and

00:10:50.089 --> 00:10:52.540
help Daniel save his business.

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:56.680
Also during Daniel's trip to Okinawa, there's
an amusing shout-out to the scene in the Karate

00:10:56.680 --> 00:11:01.029
Kid Part 2 where the young Daniel broke six
blocks of ice in a bet.

00:11:01.029 --> 00:11:04.149
This time though, the ice is just for drinks
at a bar.

00:11:04.149 --> 00:11:08.850
Season 2 already tipped its hat to that famous
scene when Daniel was about to perform a demonstration

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:13.300
to encourage new Miyagi-do students when he
was interrupted by Cobra Kai's showier demo

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.649
with fire.

00:11:14.649 --> 00:11:18.610
Kreese may have kicked Johnny out of his own
dojo at the end of season 2, but in the first

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:23.170
episode of the new season, we see that Johnny
is still taking a page out of Kreese's book.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:27.459
This time, he's putting his fist through car
windows during a fight, just like Kreese did

00:11:27.459 --> 00:11:29.350
back in The Karate Kid Part 2.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:33.540
And it's not just car windows that get smashed
this season, as during their fight in the

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:37.420
finale, Kreese throws Daniel through a windowpane
at the Cobra Kai dojo,

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:41.399
a call-back to when he turned up out of the
blue at the start of season 2 and ended up

00:11:41.399 --> 00:11:43.649
kicking Johnny into one of the dojo mirrors.

00:11:43.649 --> 00:11:47.840
All of which is a nod to Mr Miyagi's fight
with Kreese and his army pal Silver in the

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:49.339
Karate Kid Part 3.

00:11:49.339 --> 00:11:53.680
Oh, and just to confirm much Cobra Kai are
taking after their new sensei,

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:58.199
the final episode also shows the Cobra kids
throwing Bert through the kitchen window at

00:11:58.199 --> 00:11:59.590
the LaRusso home.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.610
When Kreese starts to choke Johnny during
their final face-off, that's a call-back to

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:05.209
what he did in the second film.

00:12:05.209 --> 00:12:09.040
He has actually had Johnny in a chokehold
in the series before now, though he was giving

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.899
a demo in a class at the time rather than
trying to beat him up.

00:12:12.899 --> 00:12:17.170
Given he's such a fan of The Iron Eagle movie,
it's no wonder Johnny takes Miguel to a gig

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:18.949
by Dee Snider this season.

00:12:18.949 --> 00:12:23.329
After all, "We're Not Gonna Take It", a song
by Snider's former heavy metal band Twisted

00:12:23.329 --> 00:12:25.149
Sister features in that film.

00:12:25.149 --> 00:12:30.269
And the Cobra Kai series itself used the same
song in season 1 during Johnny's unconventional

00:12:30.269 --> 00:12:32.930
training with his students for the tournament.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:36.920
When Miguel takes photos of Johnny for his
Facebook page, look at the book he's pretending

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:37.920
to read.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.220
It's called The Queen of Hearts, which is
fitting because Johnny's having these photos

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.990
taken to impress his former teen love, Ali.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:48.361
But also the book's about two long-time friends
who are forced to re-assess their lives when

00:12:48.361 --> 00:12:50.649
an old flame returns out of the blue.

00:12:50.649 --> 00:12:54.820
Now, Daniel and Johnny may not be friends
exactly but they do go way back and their

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:59.449
old flame Ali turning up all of a sudden does
force them to confront the truth about their

00:12:59.449 --> 00:13:00.560
past and themselves.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.560
"You guys are more alike than you want to
admit."

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:07.570
After the car wash fundraiser, the Cobra Kai
bullies wait for Nathaniel to be by himself

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:11.019
on his push-bike then arrive on their motorbikes
to beat him up.

00:13:11.019 --> 00:13:15.260
Which is a throwback to when Johnny and his
Cobra Kai buds got Daniel when he was alone

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:20.220
on his bicycle and roughed him up by using
their motorbikes to drive him off the track.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:24.470
After the big fight between the kids at Golf
'n' Stuff, Amanda confronts Kreese at his

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:25.470
dojo:
"Hey, Rambo.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:26.470
Kids are getting hurt."

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.220
Calling Kreese 'Rambo', of course, fits his
tough, aggressive character and his Vietnam

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:35.170
background, but it's also a nod to the fact
that actor Martin Kove appeared in the second

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:36.829
film in that franchise.

00:13:36.829 --> 00:13:40.720
When Daniel's scrolling through family photos
on his phone at the bar in Japan, I think

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:45.009
this picture here is Ralph Macchio with his
real-life daughter Julia.

00:13:45.009 --> 00:13:49.660
And at the city council meeting, this looks
like a cameo from Macchio's actual son Daniel.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:54.860
When the show first introduces Kreese's army
buddies, everything about this guy here points

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:58.279
to him being Terry Silver,
the main villain in the third movie, from

00:13:58.279 --> 00:14:02.020
his ponytail to the shape of his face, his
whole manner and the way he talks.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:03.080
"Johnny, Johnny!"

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.080
"Hey!

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:05.080
Johnny!

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:06.080
Johnny, Johnny!"

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:10.980
So, it's a bit of a shock when he's killed
off and the other guy Twig is revealed as

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:11.980
the real Silver!

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:13.620
"Shut your goddamn mouth, Silver!"

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:16.070
And Twig's line in the show,
'I owe you man',

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:18.760
echoes his words to Kreese in the third movie:
'I owe you, man.'

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:23.779
And now, with Kreese's phone call in the finale,
we look set to see the original actor who

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:28.220
played Silver in the movie, Thomas Ian Griffith,
back on screen with his Cobra Kai chum.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:32.190
And I talk more about that in my Season 3
Ending Explained video.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:35.649
Tap here to watch that or follow the link
in the video description.

00:14:35.649 --> 00:14:40.399
And it's also interesting how the finale uses
the classic 80s song 'In The Air Tonight'

00:14:40.399 --> 00:14:43.519
to underscore the dramatic build-up to next
season's showdown,

00:14:43.519 --> 00:14:44.949
with the track's famous drum roll signalling
the arrival of Johnny in a moment that couldn't

00:14:44.949 --> 00:14:49.360
be more 1980s if he was wearing an I Heart
the 80s hoodie and headband.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:53.759
The lyrics, 'I've been waiting for this moment
all my life', are especially fitting and neatly

00:14:53.759 --> 00:14:57.620
meta as not only has Kreese been craving revenge
for years,

00:14:57.620 --> 00:15:02.329
but many fans of the show have also been hoping
for a team-up between Daniel and Johnny!

00:15:02.329 --> 00:15:07.209
I also like the way the finale subtly tips
it hat to Daniel and Johnny's showdown at

00:15:07.209 --> 00:15:12.090
the end of the first Karate Kid film by having
Daniel dressed in white and Johnny in black.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:16.730
So, what other easter eggs, call-backs or
awesome details did you spot this season?

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.730
And what was your favourite moment?

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:22.959
Comment below and if you enjoyed this leave
a thumbs-up, I really appreciate it.

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:27.149
Tap left for my next Cobra Kai video or tap
right for something else you're sure to like.

00:15:27.149 --> 00:15:29.350
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.390
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

